{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== VERDICT ON HARNESS RUN 30767546938: PRODUCT DEFECT, NOT A SETUP ARTIFACT. Filed as\n[rebar:8d68-a740-4f56-4b3a], parent e369, linked discovered_from 5200. ===\n\nTHE CELL IS WORKING. Run 30767546938 (job 91548842532, main @ d72c4777d3, 48 passed / 1 failed,\ncanary collected=49 executed=49 skipped=0): both new setup assertions PASSED — the local ticket\ncleared after the inbound unassign, and jira/'admin' resolved to None after the cell removed the\nmapping — so the cell reached its real assertion for the first time and reported\n\"THE PASS MINTED NOTHING\". That is the oracle discriminating, exactly as built.\n\nTHE SUSPICION WAS THAT MY SETUP LEFT THE PASS NOTHING TO MINT ON. It did not, and that is\nmeasured rather than argued. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_inbound_update_assignee_mint_5200.py`\ncell 1 runs the REAL `inbound_differ._diff_jira_vs_local` on exactly the state the cell arranges\n— local ticket unassigned, Jira issue assigned to admin — and the differ DOES emit\n`changed[\"assignee\"]`. The mint is reached. It declines the value it is handed.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, each link cited, all four cells green harness-free:\n 1. `inbound_fields._map_jira_to_local_fields:223-225` emits TWO keys from the Jira user object:\n    the scalar `assignee` (a bare STRING — `_extract_jira_field_value` takes `name`, else\n    `displayName`) and the fixed-shape `assignee_identity` whose `account_id` ALREADY carries\n    DC's username (`_identity_of:154-179`, the precedence 5f48 established).\n 2. `inbound_differ._diff_jira_vs_local:185-188` forwards ONLY the scalar.\n    `assignee_identity` is not in `field_map` (`:170-176`), so it never reaches the mutation.\n 3. `apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_write_edit_event:333-340` says these fields ARE the\n    differ's local-keyed shape, and `:369` passes `fields[\"assignee\"]` — the string — to the mint.\n 4. `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity:100-101` opens `if not isinstance(assignee, dict): return`.\n    A string returns before any key is read. Nothing minted, nothing logged: `:83-102` documents\n    the function as best-effort with every failure swallowed.\n\nWHY NOBODY SAW IT: the CREATE path (`:200-203`) passes the RAW user object from the snapshot,\nwhich IS a dict, so an inbound create mints correctly. Every identity this harness has ever\nproduced came from that path — including the one that made this cell's ORIGINAL precondition\nfail two runs ago. The update path has never minted, on either deployment.\n\nRECONCILING THE LOCAL GREEN WITH THE LIVE RED (change 1216/1227's mutation suite passes): that\nsuite drives `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity` with a DICT — the create path's shape, chosen\ndeliberately as \"the function the inbound apply actually calls\". It is; but on the UPDATE path it\nis called with a string. The new module's cells 2 and 3 are that exact contrast: same function,\nsame store, same user, one variable — dict mints, string does not.\n\nNOT A REGRESSION OF 5f48-244c-7063-42d0. That fixed the KEY SELECTION inside the mint\n(`for key in (\"accountId\", \"name\")`). This is upstream of that guard: the update path returns at\nline 100 before any key is read, so 5f48's fix is never reached. A case it never covered.\n\nPROPOSED FIX — NOT IMPLEMENTED, awaiting sign-off; no src/ change made. Plumb the identity\nthrough: have the differ forward `jira_mapped[\"assignee_identity\"]` alongside the scalar (the\nmapper already computes it), and key the mint on that. DO NOT loosen the dict guard to accept a\nbare string: on DC the scalar is `name` (== the external id) so it would look right, but on Cloud\nit is the DISPLAY NAME, and minting keyed on that registers every Cloud user under the wrong\nexternal id — silently worse than the missing mint. Cell 4 pins the contract a correct fix meets.\n\nDiagnosis test landed for review as Gerrit change (test-tier only, `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/`)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785709756286069001,"uuid":"0bd8c7e2-d5a2-42b8-bb6b-ae2008a83c98"}